        "link": "https://www.scmp.com/news/hong-kong/hong-kong-economy/article/3357338/hong-kong-must-stay-outward-looking-and-free-port-thrive-trader-leaders-say?utm_source=rss_feed",
        "orario": "16:03",
        "timestamp": 1781618606,
        "contenuto_esteso": "Advertisement Hong Kong economy Hong Kong Hong Kong Economy Hong Kong must stay outward-looking and a free port to thrive, trade leaders say Former and current Trade Development Council leaders stress free flow of goods, people and capital, while deepening ties with China amid geopolitical tensions 2 -MIN READ 2 -MIN Listen Ambrose Li Published: 10:03pm, 16 Jun 2026 Updated: 10:32pm, 16 Jun 2026 Hong Kong must remain an outward-looking economy and a free port to thrive, current and former leaders of the city’s trade promotion body have said, stressing the importance of working with mainland China and tackling challenges creatively. Hong Kong Trade Development Council chairman Frederick Ma Si-hang and four of his predecessors spoke on Tuesday at a panel discussion marking the organisation’s 60th anniversary. “Hong Kong has to maintain its outward-looking society and economy,” former chairman Victor Fung Kwok-king said. Advertisement “The council plays a vital role in doing so, and it could manifest in many forms. The important part is maintaining this positioning.” Fellow former chairman Peter Woo Kwong-ching warned against a repeat of pandemic-era restrictions, urging Hong Kong to safeguard its core freedoms and that the council should press the government against any future shutdowns. Advertisement “Hong Kong is a free port with a free flow of goods, people, capital and information. We must keep these four,” he said. “The shutdown of Hong Kong during Covid-19 must not happen again. Advertisement Select Voice Select Speed 00:00 00:00 1.00"
